slides=[
('Cadence: text analysis and PII detection','A local document-review pipeline for financial-services evidence.','The problem: personal information must be removed before document text reaches an AI model.\nOur approach: extract, classify, mask, verify, then allow or block export.\nWalkthrough: business need → design → component roles → demonstration → evidence.', 'Open with the business constraint. PII means personally identifiable information. We built a local demonstration; we did not integrate an external LLM. Explain that the deck has a main walkthrough and deeper interview reference slides.'),
('The problem we were given','Useful business context must survive; personal details must not.','Cadence employees use documents to support operational decisions.\nEvidence arrives as scanned policies, organizational presentations and process documents.\nThe case requires repeatable extraction, PII classification and redaction before downstream AI processing.', 'An organizational chart can contain names and email addresses while a policy scan can hide identifiers in screenshots. A single regular expression on the visible document text would not cover both.'),
('What the judges asked to see','Five outcomes shape the design.','Solution blueprint: the stages and data flow from upload to detection output.\nSystem architecture: technical components and how they interact.\nExtraction logic: how each format is read and PII is classified.\nReliable outcomes: ≥80% structure/context and near-complete PII coverage targets.\nLive walkthrough: upload the actual artifacts and inspect the results.', 'These are requirements, not achieved accuracy claims. The original brief asks for a 10–15 minute walkthrough. Use the deeper slides as backup when judges ask questions.'),
('Three inputs, three extraction challenges','The supplied files are the evidence, not interchangeable samples.','Organizational PPTX: 9 slides with charts, directories and contact details.\nRisk policy PDF: 35 scanned pages, including tables and embedded screenshots.\nTPRM training DOCX: 16 tables and 41 media entries, including vector formats.\nThe shared difficulty is preserving relationships while reading all relevant text.', 'TPRM means third-party risk management. Native text is directly encoded text; a scanned image requires OCR. Word pagination is not fixed in XML, so we do not invent page numbers.'),
('The complete journey','Every stage contributes evidence to the final release decision.','Upload → Validate → Extract / OCR → Detect → Mask → Verify\nPASS: download checked plain text.\nREVIEW: inspect warnings locally; export remains blocked.\nThe original document and image bytes are not included in the text download.', 'Walk from left to right. Validation asks whether the file is legitimate and readable. Extraction asks whether the content was recovered. Detection finds sensitive spans. Redaction replaces them. Verification checks both content and completeness.'),
('How the code fits together','The runtime connects the merged components through explicit adapters.','Browser + web.py → local session, upload, polling and download routes.\nruntime.py + ingestion/orchestrator.py → validation, stage order and status.\nextraction package → PDF / OOXML readers and local OCR.\ncomprehensive.py → local NER, contextual rules and validators.\nredactor.py + gate.py → span masking, rescan and release decision.', 'An adapter translates between component contracts. Here it reconciles identifiers, confidence scales, metadata and findings. The legacy orchestrator has fail-closed stub tests; the real app and CLI inject actual adapters.'),
('Role: platform and integration','Make independent components behave as one predictable pipeline.','Own the lifecycle: validation → extraction → detection → redaction → verification.\nNormalize document IDs, block references, source locations and confidence values.\nCarry warnings forward instead of turning incomplete work into a success.\nReturn one response contract for the browser, CLI and acceptance runner.', 'Input is a path or uploaded bytes. Output is a pipeline response plus restricted local context. SHA-based identifiers help maintain document identity. Missing stages, unsupported files and adapter failures must block release.'),
('Role: document extraction and OCR','Recover text with enough location information to review it.','Prefer native text; use local OCR for scans and embedded images.\nPreserve ordered blocks, table indices, source paths and word boxes.\nHandle supported SVG / EMF media through local conversion.\nReport unreadable regions, low confidence and incomplete extraction.\nDo not equate “page processed” with “all information recovered”.', 'OCR means optical character recognition. PDFium renders pages and Tesseract recognizes text. A block stores text together with its source reference. On a chart, reading order may not fully preserve visual relationships; this needs manual comparison.'),
('Extraction differs by format','The reader follows the file structure rather than treating every file as a page.','PDF: recover native text where available; render and OCR scanned content.\nPPTX: inspect shapes, tables, notes and relevant packaged media.\nDOCX: inspect paragraphs, tables, headers / footers and embedded media.\nKeep neighboring context and exact text offsets for later detection.\nRetain warnings when conversion or recognition is uncertain.', 'Explain OOXML as the zipped XML package used by modern Word and PowerPoint. Source paths and table coordinates are useful even when a fixed page number is unavailable. Crops can help small screenshot text, but OCR still needs review.'),
('Role: PII detection and classification','Use several signals because personal information has different shapes.','Local named-entity recognition identifies likely people in natural language.\nContextual rules identify labels, contact details and organization-specific IDs.\nValidators check dates and payment-card checksums where applicable.\nDocument-local aliases connect repeated names and initials.\nEach finding keeps a type, block reference and exact character span.', 'NER is named-entity recognition, using spaCy and the local English model. Regex is useful for formatted identifiers but weak for names. Context reduces false positives, though heuristics and OCR noise can still cause errors.'),
('What we classify','Fourteen categories cover the implemented text recognizers.','Identity: person, employee ID, director ID, vendor-person ID.\nContact: email, phone, home address.\nPersonal: date of birth, national ID, tax ID, passport.\nFinancial: bank account, payment card, partial identifier.\nRepeated mentions count separately; business dates and control IDs are negative examples.', 'Distinguish PII from any number or capitalized phrase. An invoice amount is not automatically a bank account; a policy approval date is not automatically a DOB. The supported category list is not proof of complete global coverage.'),
('One example from text to checked output','Synthetic example: keep the action, remove the identity.','INPUT   Contact Noor Example at noor@example.invalid for the review.\nFIND    PERSON: Noor Example | EMAIL: noor@example.invalid\nMASK    Contact [PERSON_1] at [EMAIL_1] for the review.\nCHECK   Rescan the result and check known sensitive spans were removed.\nRESULT  The review instruction remains understandable.', 'This is a synthetic teaching example, not a quotation from the supplied business evidence. Tokens are stable within a document. Exact offsets prevent masking unrelated text; identifiers are not shared across documents.'),
('Role: redaction and release controls','A finding is only useful if masking cannot leave a sensitive tail behind.','Validate span types, bounds, confidence and document / block identity.\nMerge crossing and transitive overlaps into their full covered range.\nApply replacements from right to left to preserve original offsets.\nKeep typed placeholders consistent within the document.\nExclude reversible mappings and raw OCR metadata from public results.', 'Example: spans 2–8 and 6–12 must mask 2–12, not discard one span and expose its tail. Applying a replacement at the end first leaves earlier character indices unchanged. Invalid spans raise errors rather than being silently counted as masked.'),
('The release gate','“No findings” is not enough to approve a document.','Rescan masked text with the integrated detector and independent patterns.\nCheck known original sensitive spans for surviving values.\nRequire complete extraction, available recognizers and acceptable quality.\nBlock downloads for unresolved PII, missing models or incomplete content.\nKeep a local preview available so a reviewer can investigate.', 'A failed OCR engine could otherwise produce empty text and falsely look safe. The gate combines content checks with evidence that required processing actually completed. It cannot prove that an unknown PII type was never present.'),
('Role: application and reviewer experience','Show enough information for a person to understand the decision.','Upload a real PDF, PPTX or DOCX, with a 50 MB limit.\nFollow stage progress; inspect categories, locations and masked text.\nExpand extraction notes and original-text inspection only when needed.\nEnable checked-text download only for approved jobs.\nUse a local session token and validate host / origin on API requests.', 'The UI is served by a loopback-only Python server. No external assets or model requests are needed for uploads. Original inspection is intentionally restricted because it contains raw PII. Local demonstration controls are not a full internet deployment security model.'),
('Role: evaluation and quality assurance','Measure the result against labels that were created independently.','Precision = correct detections / all detections.\nRecall = correct detections / all labeled sensitive occurrences.\nF1 balances precision and recall; exact matching checks type and span.\nStructure review checks headings, order, tables and neighboring context.\nReport the dataset, denominator and known limitations with every claim.', 'A false positive is ordinary text marked sensitive. A false negative is a missed sensitive occurrence. Predictions must not be used as their own ground truth. The structure rubric is our proposed measurement method, not a formula specified in the brief.'),
('What the tests currently establish','Passing checks support specific claims, not universal accuracy.','288 regression tests passed, with no failures, errors or skips.\n12 acceptance checks passed for valid and invalid file workflows.\nThe labeled synthetic fixture: 18/18 exact matches, zero false positives.\nBrowser checks verified upload, progress, download and blocked export.\nFull-source recall and ≥80% structure still require independent review.', 'The counts are the local verification recorded on 14 October 2026. Synthetic accuracy is bounded to that fixture. The actual scanned policy and training document can remain quarantined because OCR or rescan issues require human investigation.'),
('The cases we deliberately challenge','Test failure paths as carefully as the happy path.','Files: valid, empty, corrupt, encrypted, unsupported and mismatched type.\nDetection: Unicode offsets, aliases, repeated mentions and business negatives.\nMasking: invalid spans, overlapping ranges and immutable findings.\nGate: missing NER, incomplete extraction and residual identifiers.\nWeb: unauthorized access, cross-origin requests and blocked downloads.', 'Explain why testing only valid fixtures would be misleading. Each class targets a way that sensitive data could slip through or the UI could falsely suggest approval. No finite suite covers every future document.'),
('What changed during integration','The difficult work was joining real behavior and preserving failure evidence.','Connected real readers and recognizers instead of a fixture-only flow.\nTranslated confidence scales and normalized IDs across component boundaries.\nFixed overlap masking, invalid spans and raw metadata exposure.\nExpanded verification beyond simple email and phone patterns.\nMade missing dependencies and incomplete extraction block export.', 'Use a concrete before/after: an overlap could previously leave part of an identifier; union masking removes the whole range. Adapters resolved valid components disagreeing about formats. Distinguish initial role ownership from these integration fixes.'),
('Live demonstration','Show one approved path and one review path.','Start: python scripts/run_app.py --port 8767.\nOpen http://127.0.0.1:8767 and upload the organizational PPTX.\nExplain a detected type, its location and its masked text.\nShow checked download when approved; then show a blocked review case.\nFor long scans, prepare a completed real run and disclose that it is pre-run.', 'Allow 2–3 minutes. Do not promise that every provided file will approve. Start OCR-heavy jobs before presenting. If Python is not on PATH, use the workstation bundled Python command from the README. Keep real personal text off the projector when possible.'),
('Limits and next steps','The remaining work is evidence and review, not a claim of perfect detection.','Adjudicate full-source PII labels and inspect missed / false detections.\nScore structure against original pages, charts and table relationships.\nImprove OCR for small screenshots and unfamiliar document layouts.\nText export excludes visual PII; original-file redaction is a separate feature.\nProduction hosting needs authentication, TLS, isolation and retention controls.', 'Do not lower OCR thresholds merely to obtain an approval. The case goals remain targets until independent measurements support them. Photo and signature recognition is not implemented as a full visual PII system.'),
('Interview reference: why these choices?','Explain the tradeoff, then give a concrete example.','Why local? Raw evidence stays on the workstation.\nWhy native text first? It avoids unnecessary OCR errors.\nWhy rules + NER? Formatting and language need different signals.\nWhy text-only output? It avoids retaining hidden original image layers.\nWhy block uncertainty? An unreadable page cannot establish privacy.', 'If asked why not use an LLM for raw PII detection, return to the problem constraint: raw PII must be stripped before LLM processing. Local recognizers keep that boundary. Text-only output sacrifices original layout fidelity; be explicit about that limitation.'),
('Interview reference: explain your component','A useful answer follows input → work → output → failure → test.','Platform: file / bytes → stage coordination → one response contract.\nExtraction: document → ordered text blocks + locations + warnings.\nDetection: blocks → typed findings with exact spans.\nRedaction / application: findings → masked preview + enforced release state.\nQA: independent labels + outputs → metrics and reproducible evidence.', 'Each contributor should explain the code they actually own, without claiming every module. Mention a real failure case and the test that demonstrates it. Use the role labels rather than numbered people.'),
('Closing: a reviewable privacy boundary','The system makes the decision visible and keeps uncertain evidence local.','We can demonstrate actual uploads, local extraction and classified PII.\nMasked text preserves the surrounding instruction where extraction succeeds.\nExport is conditional on verification and completeness.\nOur next milestone is independently measured coverage on the full evidence set.', 'Close in thirty seconds. Invite questions on extraction, detector tradeoffs, the release gate or evaluation. Avoid saying the project guarantees 100% privacy.')]
